<div data-figma-id="232:65" data-design-page="about" class="d-6e4b511eb">
  <div data-figma-id="249:73" class="d-f4802d4d6">
    <div data-figma-id="450:361" class="d-116c02f58">
      <div data-figma-id="450:362" class="d-eb5fa5b72">
        <p data-figma-id="450:363" class="d-3848c34e5">
          ABOUT / ROMINA VEISY
        </p>
        <div data-figma-id="450:364" class="d-5ec8b666b">
          <p data-figma-id="450:365" class="d-76b62571f">
            UX / PRODUCT DESIGNER
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="450:366" class="d-697cfc35c">
            ARCHITECTURE → SERVICE → DIGITAL PRODUCTS
          </p>
        </div>
        <h1 class="about-headline">
          <span data-figma-id="450:367" class="d-756eb0c2b">
            I learned to design spaces.
          </span>
          <span data-figma-id="450:368" class="d-756eb0c2b">
            Now I design the systems
          </span>
          <span data-figma-id="450:369" class="d-3a0904ea8">
            people move through.
          </span>
        </h1>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="450:370" class="d-5a8e20f5b">
        <div data-figma-id="450:371" class="d-67cd8a30e">
          <div data-figma-id="450:372" class="d-8bdc9dc88">
            <div data-figma-id="450:374" class="d-842238ae0">
              <img
                alt="Black-and-white portrait of Romina Veisy, with a hillside town behind her"
                src="/images/about/portrait-original-black-and-white-unedited-1eebc.webp"
                width="1117"
                height="1408"
                loading="eager"
                decoding="async"
                class="d-ab01af0bc"
              />
            </div>
          </div>
          <p data-figma-id="450:376" class="d-83c91cf79">
            ROMINA VEISY — ’S-HERTOGENBOSCH, THE NETHERLANDS
          </p>
        </div>
        <div data-figma-id="450:377" class="d-98c5cf202">
          <h2 data-figma-id="450:378" class="d-56d633ffe">
            I did not leave architecture behind. I brought its way of thinking with me.
          </h2>
          <p data-figma-id="450:379" class="d-b64085840">
            I trained as an architect in Iran, where design was never only about appearance. A plan
            had to hold people, movement, structure, materials, regulations and time at once. My
            bachelor thesis explored a residential complex for orphans—an environment designed
            around vitality, creativity and belonging.
          </p>
          <p data-figma-id="450:380" class="d-b64085840">
            Working in interior design made that responsibility immediate: decisions became rooms
            people lived in, details contractors had to build, and compromises that still needed to
            feel intentional. Later, service design showed me that the invisible parts of an
            experience—handoffs, queues, routines and ownership—shape people just as much as walls
            do.
          </p>
          <h2 data-figma-id="450:381" class="d-70cccec14">
            Today I design digital products and visual systems. The medium changed; the question did
            not: how can a complex system become clear enough for someone to move through it with
            confidence?
          </h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="450:382" class="d-5221b5460">
        <div data-figma-id="450:383" class="d-884178a9f"></div>
        <p data-figma-id="450:384" class="d-ef174e4cb">
          01 / THE THREAD BETWEEN THE WORK
        </p>
        <h2 data-figma-id="450:385" class="d-846f44562">
          Different mediums. One way of seeing.
        </h2>
        <p data-figma-id="450:386" class="d-be4d64ab6">
          My background is not a collection of separate careers. Each step taught me to see a
          different layer of the same problem: people trying to act inside systems they did not
          design.
        </p>
        <div data-figma-id="450:387" class="d-9be409e26">
          <div data-figma-id="450:388" class="d-1e7f430fd">
            <p data-figma-id="450:389" class="d-3b9eb3b40">
              01
            </p>
            <p data-figma-id="450:390" class="d-9594ed484">
              Architecture
            </p>
            <h2 data-figma-id="450:391" class="d-557ef663f">
              See the whole system
            </h2>
            <p data-figma-id="450:392" class="d-f2a15a889">
              I learned to think in relationships: people and space, form and structure, ambition
              and constraint. A strong idea has to survive reality.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div data-figma-id="450:393" class="d-1e7f430fd">
            <p data-figma-id="450:394" class="d-3b9eb3b40">
              02
            </p>
            <p data-figma-id="450:395" class="d-9594ed484">
              Service design
            </p>
            <h2 data-figma-id="450:396" class="d-557ef663f">
              Find what happens between moments
            </h2>
            <p data-figma-id="450:397" class="d-f2a15a889">
              At Albert Heijn, the customer experience depended on layouts, operations and teams
              moving together. The visible moment was only the surface.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div data-figma-id="450:398" class="d-1e7f430fd">
            <p data-figma-id="450:399" class="d-3b9eb3b40">
              03
            </p>
            <p data-figma-id="450:400" class="d-9594ed484">
              Product design
            </p>
            <h2 data-figma-id="450:401" class="d-557ef663f">
              Make complexity usable
            </h2>
            <p data-figma-id="450:402" class="d-f2a15a889">
              At Cyclomedia, I turn technical workflows, system architecture and geospatial
              information into tools and stories people can understand and use.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="450:403" class="d-5221b5460">
        <div data-figma-id="450:404" class="d-884178a9f"></div>
        <p data-figma-id="450:405" class="d-ef174e4cb">
          02 / HOW I WORK
        </p>
        <h2 data-figma-id="450:406" class="d-846f44562">
          When the answer is not obvious.
        </h2>
        <h2 data-figma-id="450:407" class="d-a0e49db7a">
          I start before the interface and stay until the consequence is clear.
        </h2>
        <div data-figma-id="450:408" class="d-11fac39be">
          <div data-figma-id="450:409" class="d-6bb6c9eec">
            <p data-figma-id="450:410" class="d-e046e105f">
              01
            </p>
            <h2 data-figma-id="450:411" class="d-3d88001bf">
              Find the real system
            </h2>
            <p data-figma-id="450:412" class="d-359b7db30">
              I look beyond the brief to the people, rules, handoffs and incentives surrounding it.
              The right screen cannot repair the wrong system.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div data-figma-id="450:413" class="d-43def4db9"></div>
          <div data-figma-id="450:414" class="d-6bb6c9eec">
            <p data-figma-id="450:415" class="d-e046e105f">
              02
            </p>
            <h2 data-figma-id="450:416" class="d-3d88001bf">
              Make complexity visible
            </h2>
            <p data-figma-id="450:417" class="d-359b7db30">
              Flows, prototypes, diagrams and motion help teams see the same problem. Once the
              invisible becomes visible, decisions get sharper.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div data-figma-id="450:418" class="d-43def4db9"></div>
          <div data-figma-id="450:419" class="d-6bb6c9eec">
            <p data-figma-id="450:420" class="d-e046e105f">
              03
            </p>
            <h2 data-figma-id="450:421" class="d-3d88001bf">
              Test where life happens
            </h2>
            <p data-figma-id="450:422" class="d-359b7db30">
              A design should work outside the presentation: on the street, in a busy operation,
              with incomplete information and real constraints.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div data-figma-id="450:423" class="d-43def4db9"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="450:424" class="d-5221b5460">
        <div data-figma-id="450:425" class="d-884178a9f"></div>
        <p data-figma-id="450:426" class="d-ef174e4cb">
          03 / NOW
        </p>
        <h2 data-figma-id="450:427" class="d-846f44562">
          Designing tools—and the stories that make them matter.
        </h2>
        <div data-figma-id="450:428" class="d-9be409e26">
          <div data-figma-id="450:429" class="d-f68cf0e84">
            <p data-figma-id="450:430" class="d-2d6145c14">
              CYCLOMEDIA
            </p>
            <h2 data-figma-id="450:431" class="d-65fc0e0d9">
              Internal product systems
            </h2>
            <p data-figma-id="450:432" class="d-31d26c79b">
              I design UX flows, interactive prototypes and data-visualisation tools that turn dense
              operational and technical information into decisions people can make quickly.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div data-figma-id="450:433" class="d-f68cf0e84">
            <p data-figma-id="450:434" class="d-2d6145c14">
              SAMENSTAD
            </p>
            <h2 data-figma-id="450:435" class="d-65fc0e0d9">
              A civic product from the ground up
            </h2>
            <p data-figma-id="450:436" class="d-31d26c79b">
              Alongside my professional work, I am co-building a civic reporting platform with my
              co-founder. It asks how residents, municipalities and companies can make a broken
              street visible, accountable and fixable together.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div data-figma-id="450:437" class="d-fb9d3c67e">
        <div data-figma-id="450:438" class="d-884178a9f"></div>
        <p data-figma-id="450:439" class="d-ef174e4cb">
          04 / OUTSIDE THE SCREEN
        </p>
        <h2 data-figma-id="450:440" class="d-a07a54ef2">
          Still curious. Still making.
        </h2>
        <p data-figma-id="450:441" class="d-3f3642aff">
          Away from product work, I keep returning to a camera, watercolour and physical models.
          They slow me down in different ways: photography trains attention, painting makes room for
          intuition, and model-making forces an idea to meet gravity. Click any image to see it
          larger.
        </p>
        <div data-figma-id="470:361" class="d-6b25a1961">
          <div data-figma-id="470:362" class="d-4115fcc65">
